/// \defgroup grp_memory Memory
/// \brief Heaps and allocators: the exported `MemoryPool` API, its
///        `GeneralAllocator` heaps, global `operator new` and `delete`, and
///        the smaller pool allocators.
///
/// Global `operator new` and `delete`, the C `free` replacement and the
/// default STLport byte allocator all end in this group's code. The
/// exported `MemoryPool` functions keep a default heap plus a few named
/// heaps, chosen by a short character-code id; two small wrappers reach the
/// default heap directly. Smaller pool allocators sit in front of them,
/// among them WWLib's fixed-size object pools, which take their blocks from
/// the STLport byte allocator. Apt's DOGMA pools serve small requests,
/// rounded up to a multiple of four bytes, from per-size free lists. The
/// game fills Apt's allocation and free callbacks with the global array
/// `operator new[]` and `operator delete[]`, so the pools' memory comes from
/// the `MemoryPool` heaps.
/// \ref sub_memory describes the subsystem, its startup and its differences
/// from Zero Hour.
